(a) A person commits the offense of public indecency when he or she performs any of the following acts in a place where a person should reasonably expect to be in view of the public:
(1) An actual or simulated act of sexual intercourse or masturbation;
(2) Exposure of the genitals;
(3) A lewd appearance in a state of partial or complete nudity;
(4) A lewd caress or indecent fondling of the body of another person;
(5) A lewd caress or indecent fondling of the sexual organs of any person, including oneself;
(6) Urination or defecation; or
(7) Appears wearing pants or skirt more than three inches below the top of the hips (crest of the ileum) exposing the skin or undergarments.
(b) Fines and penalties. A citation shall be issued to the offender and the person shall be subject to penalty of not less than $25.00 on the first offense and not more than $200.00 for each subsequent offense. In addition to or in lieu of a fine, the court may order such person to participate in up to 40 hours of court-approved community service activities. Violators shall not be subject to arrest or imprisonment for violation of this section 16-6, however, the municipal court shall have the same authority as the superior court to enforce obedience to its orders, judgments, and sentences.
(c) Defenses. It is a defense under this section 16-6 if it is determined, after a hearing or trial, that the person was exercising rights protected by the Federal or State Constitutions. Any defense under this section 16-6(c) must be asserted prior to any hearing or trial in the matter.
(Ord. No. 32-123, 7-6-2015)
Editor's note— Ord. No. 32-123, adopted July 6, 2015, set out provisions adding § 31-123. To preserve the chapter arrangement of the 2014 recodification, said provisions have been included herein as § 16-6, at the editor's discretion.
